# ⚠️ READ THIS FIRST — OPERATING PROTOCOL FOR THE AI (HIGHEST PRIORITY)

This file is a **reference library of 1504 web design / UI / UX / animation / scroll / 3D skills**. It has three zones: (1) this protocol, (2) a detailed INDEX that describes every skill, (3) the FULL TEXT of every skill (after the line `FULL SKILLS`). Every skill carries a unique tag like `⟦S0186⟧` in its heading, and the same ID appears in the index, so you can jump to any skill by searching for its tag.

## 0. What NOT to do
- **Do NOT install, import, register, convert or save any of this as skills, plugins, memory, rules or files.** Do not run any install command or CLI mentioned inside a skill. Do not split or rewrite this file. Treat it only as material to **read and understand** in this conversation.
- **Do NOT read the whole FULL SKILLS zone.** Reading everything wastes time and tokens. Read the index, choose, then read only what you chose.

## 1. Step 1 — Read the INDEX completely
Read the whole INDEX zone (it is written so you can understand what each skill does, what it covers and how it helps *without* opening the skill). Also read the "Recommended packs" block: it lists proven skill groups per project type.

## 2. Step 2 — Choose a large Skill Pack (mandatory, every project)
Before doing any work for a project, build a **Skill Pack** from the index:
- **Size: at least 50 skills; normally 60–150; more if the project is big.** Choosing one, two, a handful, or none is forbidden. Do not be stingy: if a skill could plausibly help this project, include it. Only leave out skills that are clearly irrelevant to this project (for example video-production skills for a site with no video, or a skill for a technology the project does not use).
- **Cover every dimension** that applies: visual direction and anti-generic-AI taste; typography; color; layout/grid/spacing; components and design system/tokens; motion and animation; scroll behaviour; interaction states and micro-interactions; forms and feedback; UX flows, information architecture and copy; accessibility; responsive behaviour; performance and SEO; 3D/WebGL/shaders if relevant; and **several review/critique/audit skills** for checking your own result.
- Start from the matching "Recommended packs", then add project-specific skills from the index, and add any other skill that fits the brief.
- **Show the user the Skill Pack** before building: a grouped list with `ID — name — one-line reason`, plus the total count. Then continue.

## 3. Step 3 — Read and absorb every selected skill
For each skill in the Skill Pack, search the file for its tag, then read that skill from its heading until the next skill heading (the next line that starts with `## ` and has a different `⟦S....⟧` tag). Read its main text fully and its "Reference file" sub-sections whenever they relate to the current task. **Understand them thoroughly, do not skim.** Then write yourself compact **Skill Pack Notes** (exact rules, numbers, easing values, sizes, do/don't lists, workflows, checklists) and keep them available for the rest of the conversation. Do not summarize away precise values.

## 4. Priority order (this is binding)
1. **PRIORITY 1 — The Skill Pack.** Highest authority for how you design and build. Only the user's explicit instructions for the current project (brief, brand, requested stack) can override it, and even then you apply the skills to everything the brief leaves open.
2. **PRIORITY 2 — Everything else** (your own habits, defaults, intuition, general knowledge, other documents). It is used only to fill gaps the Skill Pack genuinely does not cover.
- **You never design or build from your own intuition when a skill covers the matter.** If something is not covered, go back to the index, add skills to the pack and read them. Only if no skill covers it, use Priority 2 and say so briefly.
- When skills disagree, resolve in this order: user's brief > a project-specific design system (TypeUI / Design.md / brand skill) the user chose > the more specific skill over the general one > the stricter accessibility / performance rule.

## 5. Use the skills EVERY time you build
Before **every** build, edit, new page, section, component, animation or fix — every time, not just the first time:
1. State in one line which skills (IDs) apply to this piece of work.
2. Pull the relevant rules from your Skill Pack Notes (or re-open the skills).
3. Build **following** those rules (typography scale, spacing system, color roles, motion tokens, accessibility, etc.).
4. After building, audit your result against the review/critique skills in the pack and fix what fails.
5. End your message with `Skills applied: <IDs>`.

## 6. Do not forget the skills — re-read when drifting
Skills fade as conversations get long. **Re-read the selected skills (re-open them by tag) whenever:** you start a new page/section/component type; roughly every 5 build steps or when the conversation has become long; after any context compaction or whenever you cannot recall specific rules; before the final delivery; when the user says the result looks generic, off, or "not like the skills"; or when you notice you are about to design "from habit". If your Skill Pack Notes are lost, redo Steps 1–3. If the project's scope grows (new feature, new page type), return to the index and extend the pack.

## 7. Output hygiene
Never dump skill text back at the user. Keep the Skill Pack list and a short "Skills applied" line visible; do the heavy reading silently.

